<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Quakers</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Religious Society of Friends, Friends</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Quakers, or Religious Society of Friends, are a Christian movement founded by George Fox in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in the 1650s. They reject clergy and formal sacraments and meet in silent worship, waiting for the 'inner light'. Persecuted in England, many emigrated to America, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-penn">William Penn</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, and Quakers became leading campaigners against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and for peace.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/quakers.jpg" alt="Quakers" width="320" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Internet Archive Book Images · No restrictions · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Religious_denominations_of_the_world_-_comprising_a_general_view_of_the_origin,_history,_and_condition_of_the_various_sects_of_Christians,_the_Jews,_and_Mahometans,_as_well_as_the_pagan_forms_of_(14761837356).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Members of the Religious Society of Friends call one another Friends, after John 15:14. The name Quakers was first used by others, because George Fox told a judge to quake before the authority of God and because critics said that they shook or trembled while preaching. The movement arose in mid-17th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> among dissenting Protestant groups that broke with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a>, many of which emerged during and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a>. In 1647 Fox claimed to have received a revelation that there is one, Christ Jesus, who can speak to a person's condition. He became convinced that a direct experience of Christ was possible without ordained clergy, and travelled through England, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbados">Barbados</a> preaching it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The early preachers, above all the group known as the Valiant Sixty, travelled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and overseas, and some were women. Quaker women published at least 220 texts in the 17th century, and Margaret Fell helped to define the movement. Persecution reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> in July 1656, when Mary Fisher and Ann Austin began preaching in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>; they were imprisoned for five weeks and banished by the Massachusetts Bay Colony. By 1700 the boisterous behaviour of the first years was no longer supported, and in the 18th century Quakers turned inward and converted fewer people.</p><p>Quakers were granted exemption from military service in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> in 1757. In the early 1700s disquiet about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> arose among Friends, many of whom in colonial America owned slaves, and the testimonies of Benjamin Lay, Anthony Benezet and John Woolman led to an abolition movement. Splits followed in America during the Revolutionary War and in the 19th century, and British Friends began missions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1866.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Friends reject priests, hold to the priesthood of all believers and mostly believe in continuing revelation. Traditionally they wore plain dress, avoided alcohol, refused to swear oaths or take part in war, and used a plain calendar of numbered days and months. In unprogrammed worship there is no plan: Friends gather in expectant waiting, and anyone moved to speak stands and does so, with silence after each message. Others hold programmed worship with hymns, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> readings and a pastor.</p><p>Decisions are taken at a meeting for worship for church business, in which members seek God's will. Quaker families founded the firms of Barclays, Lloyds, Clarks, Cadbury, Rowntree and Fry. In 1947 the British Friends Service Council and the American Friends Service Committee were awarded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Quakers” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He governed with a circle of ‘<b>Friends</b>’ (philoi), courtiers who served as generals, envoys and governors, and he relied on the loyalty of the Macedonian nobility and the citizen army.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Worship takes many forms, from the liturgies of the Orthodox and Catholic churches, with their icons, incense and ancient chants, to the simple services of the <b>Quakers</b> and the exuberant worship of Pentecostal churches, which since the early 20th century have become the fastest-growing form of Christianity.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Among free people the first sustained opposition came from the <b>Quakers</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>The Levellers, a minority movement mainly of Puritans, Presbyterians and <b>Quakers</b>, called for freedom of religion, frequent parliaments and equality under the law, and John Milton’s Areopagitica made one of the first arguments for freedom of speech.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/liberalism">Liberalism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>A tribesman who forsook his tribe for the shah alone could join the Shahsevans, the “<b>Friends</b> of the King”, 12,000 strong under Abbas.</blockquote><a href="/societies/safavid-iran">Social Hierarchy of Safavid Iran</a></li><li><blockquote>It is sung in the films The Truman Show (1998) and Fisherman's <b>Friends</b> (2019).</blockquote><a href="/songs/drunken-sailor">Drunken Sailor</a></li></ul></section>
